WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.634
At scale, your data is also a liability.

00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:09.268
One unpatched library, one plaintext password, one missing MFA prompt, and millions of records walk out the door.

00:00:09.268 --> 00:00:12.902
The rule is simple.

00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:16.536
Trust nothing.

00:00:16.536 --> 00:00:19.615
Equifax leaked a hundred forty-eight million Social Security numbers.

00:00:19.615 --> 00:00:22.695
The vector, a clever zero-day?

00:00:22.695 --> 00:00:25.774
No.

00:00:25.774 --> 00:00:28.853
An unpatched library.

00:00:28.853 --> 00:00:31.932
National Public Data left its passwords in plaintext.

00:00:31.932 --> 00:00:35.012
Change Healthcare had no MFA on a remote portal.

00:00:35.012 --> 00:00:38.091
Mundane mistakes, half a billion records between them.

00:00:38.091 --> 00:00:42.514
At the center sits the database, your crown jewels.

00:00:42.514 --> 00:00:46.938
Around it, assume every surface is already hostile.

00:00:46.938 --> 00:00:51.361
The network is sniffed, the user phished, the admin compromised, the code injected, the backups a ransomware target.

00:00:51.361 --> 00:00:55.785
Verify every request, at every one.

00:00:55.785 --> 00:00:59.770
Take the oldest attack.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.754
A user types Robert, semicolon, DROP TABLE users.

00:01:03.754 --> 00:01:07.739
Glue that into your query as text, and the database runs the DROP.

00:01:07.739 --> 00:01:11.723
Pass it as a parameter instead, and it is just a harmless name that matches nobody.

00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:15.708
Never concatenate input into SQL.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.698
SQL's power cuts both ways.

00:01:17.698 --> 00:01:19.689
One mistake hands an attacker your whole database.

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:21.679
Do not trust the network.

00:01:21.679 --> 00:01:23.669
Do not trust the user.

00:01:23.669 --> 00:01:25.659
Verify every query.

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:27.650
Next, the harder problem.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.640
Privacy.
